{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And Moses spoke to the heads of the tribes</b> – Scripture did not preface here by saying \"And YHWH spoke to Moses, saying: Speak to the heads of the tribes and say to them: This is the matter that YHWH has commanded,\" as it says in the section of those slaughtered outside (Leviticus 17:2) and in other sections, but rather it mentioned this at the end of the portion, where it says \"These are the statutes that YHWH commanded Moses\" (Numbers 30:17). And in the section of \"And it came to pass on the eighth day\" it says \"And Moses said: This is the matter that YHWH has commanded you to do\" (Leviticus 9:6), and it did not mention the command at all. And so in the section of the manna: \"And Moses said: This is the matter that YHWH has commanded: An omerful of each...\" etc. (Exodus 16:32). And \"the heads of the tribes of the children of Israel\" are the princes of the banners whom the tribes set up over their banners after Nahshon son of Amminadav and his companions died. And it is possible that they were those mentioned in the section of the inheritance: \"These are the names of the men who shall assign to you the land for an inheritance\" (Numbers 34:17), or they were others, for there Scripture speaks of the future, since at the time of the division of the land those mentioned will be alive and will stand over them. And the reason for \"And Moses spoke to the heads of the tribes\" is that there is no need to teach all the children of Israel that the father and the husband can annul vows of self-affliction, and perhaps one must conceal these statutes from them so they will not treat vows lightly, but to the sages of Israel, the heads of their tribes, he taught the law. And it further alludes to the midrash of our Sages (Nedarim 78a), that the heads of the tribes have a hand and a name in vows more than the rest of the people, for an expert individual may permit a vow. Now, the nullification of vows is not explicitly set forth in the Torah, but it is a halakhah given to Moses from Sinai. Scripture hung it by a thread of hair, as they said (Chagigah 10a): the nullification of vows fly in the air and have nothing on which to lean, except that Scripture hinted at it: \"He shall not profane his word\" (Numbers 30:3) — meaning that it did not say \"he shall not transgress his word,\" but rather commanded that he not profane his word, that he not make a profanation of the vow, for when he comes to a court and they find him an opening and he regrets it and they grant him absolution, he is not profaning it. And the reason for this is as I have said: that He made the nullification of vows and oaths like the secrets of the Torah, which are not to be told except to those worthy of them and are written only in allusion. And this portion comes here because it mentioned vows to the Most High: \"besides your vows and your freewill offerings for your burnt offerings and for your meal offerings and for your libations and for your peace offerings\" (Numbers 29:39). It further said: besides these vows that are mentioned, there are also vows of ordinary persons, and everything that proceeds from a person's mouth he is obligated to fulfill and to do all that he has forbidden upon himself, and in all of them he shall not profane his word, but others may grant him absolution."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "n/a"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:09:38.442281377Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}